+# AGENTS.md
+
+This repository is the ZBar barcode reader project. The part most relevant to day-to-day development is the video scanner path, centered on the `zbarcam` command-line application and the library code it uses.
+
+We don't apply TDD here.
+
+The project is built using Visual Studio (see visualc directory), so don't try to build automatically at all.
+
+It uses hg as the Version control system and mq patches over it. So the following should be ignored:
+- .hg directory
+- .hg.* files
+- *.orig files
+- *.rej files
+
+## High-level architecture
+
+`zbarcam` is deliberately thin. It does not implement scanning logic itself. Instead, it creates a `zbar_processor_t` from the core library and configures it to:
+
+- open a video device (for example `/dev/video0` or a Windows webcam device)
+- create a display window when enabled
+- read frames from the video backend
+- decode barcodes via the image scanner pipeline
+- emit decoded results to stdout or XML
+- optionally beep when a result is found
+
+The path is roughly:
+
+1. `zbarcam/zbarcam.c` parses arguments and sets options and  config values.
+2. `zbar_processor_create()` and `zbar_processor_init()` in `zbar/processor.c` prepare the processor.
+3. The processor uses the video subsystem in `zbar/video/` to open the device and pull frames.
+4. The frame data is fed into the scanner pipeline in `zbar/img_scanner.c`, `zbar/scanner.c`, and the decoder modules under `zbar/decoder/`.
+5. Decoded symbols are sent back through the callback registered by `zbar_processor_set_data_handler()`.
+6. The callback in `zbarcam.c` prints the symbol data and optionally emits XML.
+
+## Most relevant files
+
+### Entry point and user-facing behavior
+
+- `zbarcam/zbarcam.c` — main program entry point; CLI parsing; output formatting; main event loop
+- `zbarcam/Makefile.am.inc` — build wiring for the `zbarcam` binary; links `zbarcam/zbarcam` against `zbar/libzbar.la`
+
+### Core library and processor orchestration
+
+- `zbar/processor.c` — core `zbar_processor_t` implementation; startup, shutdown, handlers, format negotiation, input thread coordination
+- `zbar/processor.h` — processor structures and API declarations
+- `zbar/processor/posix.c` and `zbar/processor/win.c` — platform-specific processor internals
+- `zbar/video.c` and `zbar/video.h` — abstract video interface used by the processor
+- `zbar/window.c` and `zbar/window.h` — window/display support when display mode is enabled
+
+### Video backends
+
+- `zbar/video/v4l.c`, `zbar/video/v4l1.c`, `zbar/video/v4l2.c` — Linux V4L/V4L2 capture support
+- `zbar/video/null.c` — no-video fallback
+- `zbar/video/dshow.c` and related files under `zbar/video/` — Windows DirectShow path
+- `zbar/video/vfw.c` — older Windows video path
+
+These are the files that determine how frames enter the system and what device formats are supported.
+
+### Scanner and decoder pipeline
+
+- `zbar/img_scanner.c` and `zbar/img_scanner.h` — top-level image scanner used by the processor
+- `zbar/scanner.c` — low-level scan machinery and symbol extraction
+- `zbar/decoder/` — barcode decoders (EAN, Code 128, QR, etc.)
+- `zbar/symbol.c` and `zbar/symbol.h` — symbol metadata and output representation
+- `zbar/image.c` and `zbar/image.h` — image object handling and conversion
+
+### Specialized symbologies: PCard and PCard2
+
+These are not generic barcode families like EAN or QR. They are specialized playing-card barcodes used in card-dealing equipment, and they are relevant because `zbarcam` supports them as optional symbol decoders.
+
+- `zbar/decoder/pcard.c` — PCard decoder. Looks for border-bar layouts and fixed-width bar groups, then matches a 53-card code space (52 cards + joker). The implementation explains the detector in its file header: it searches for silent zones, estimates unit width, validates border widths, and emits symbols in the form `PCard:XY`.
+- `zbar/decoder/pcard2.c` — PCard2 decoder. A second playing-card format with different bar geometry: narrower, 4-bar pattern logic, no border bars, and verification of summed widths and black-bar widths before accepting a match. It emits symbols in the form `PCard2:XY`.
+- `zbar/decoder/pcard_defs.h` and `zbar/decoder/pcard2_defs.h` — canonical card-code tables and per-symbol matching data used by each decoder.
+- `zbar/decoder/pcard.c` and `zbar/decoder/pcard2.c` both emphasize that these decoders are intentionally conservative: they reject ambiguous scans and rely on uncertainty/quality thresholds to reduce false positives in webcam input.
+
+In practice, if you are debugging `zbarcam` on card-like video input, these are the places to inspect alongside the generic processor pipeline. The CLI help explicitly exposes the config hook used to enable them: `-S pcard.enable=1` and related settings, which are parsed by `zbar_processor_parse_config()` from `zbarcam/zbarcam.c`.
+
+### Public API and project integration
+
+- `include/zbar.h` — public C API used across tools and bindings
+- `include/zbar/Processor.h` — C++ wrapper around the processor API
+- `Makefile.am` — top-level build integration; includes `zbarcam/Makefile.am.inc` when video support is enabled
+- `configure.ac` — project configuration and feature toggles like `HAVE_VIDEO`, `HAVE_X`, etc.
+
+## Why this matters for zbarcam
+
+The most important fact is that `zbarcam` is not a custom webcam decoder. It is a thin application around the shared `libzbar` processor subsystem. If you are debugging webcam issues or frame-processing behavior, start from the following chain:
+
+- `zbarcam/zbarcam.c` for CLI and callback behavior
+- `zbar/processor.c` for `zbar_processor_init()` and thread lifecycle
+- `zbar/video/*` for device open/init and format negotiation
+- `zbar/img_scanner.c` for actual barcode scanning of frames
+
+## Build and feature toggles
+
+The repo is built with autotools. Relevant configuration knobs include:
+
+- `HAVE_VIDEO` — enables video capture support and the zbarcam target
+- `HAVE_X` / `HAVE_XV` — display/window support
+- `HAVE_JPEG` and image-loading features
+- `ENABLE_PCARD` / `ENABLE_PCARD2` — optional playing-card symbologies that are compiled into the core library
+- decoder feature flags in `configure.ac` and the source tree under `zbar/decoder/`
+
+The top-level build logic in `Makefile.am` adds the zbarcam binary only when video support is enabled:
+
+- `if HAVE_VIDEO`
+- `include $(srcdir)/zbarcam/Makefile.am.inc`
+
+## Typical debugging workflow
+
+When working on `zbarcam` issues, the best first read order is:
+
+1. [zbarcam/zbarcam.c](zbarcam/zbarcam.c)
+2. [zbar/processor.c](zbar/processor.c)
+3. [zbar/video.c](zbar/video.c)
+4. [zbar/img_scanner.c](zbar/img_scanner.c)
+5. the relevant backend under [zbar/video](zbar/video)
+
+This keeps the investigation focused on the actual runtime path from webcam -> frame -> decode -> output without chasing unrelated bindings or GUI code.
+
